WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.268
Connection strings should be treated as sensitive configuration.

2
00:00:04.268 --> 00:00:16.006
Even when a string does not visibly contain a password, it can reveal internal database names, provider details, routing assumptions, managed identity use, or service topology.

3
00:00:16.006 --> 00:00:21.408
It may also enable access through platform identity or integrated authentication.

4
00:00:21.408 --> 00:00:27.477
Database credentials and service accounts should be scoped to the application's real needs.

5
00:00:27.477 --> 00:00:36.480
A runtime application usually should not share the same access as migrations, administrators, reporting jobs, or emergency maintenance.

6
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.282
Separate identities make ownership and least privilege easier to review.

7
00:00:41.282 --> 00:00:45.217
Certificates and private keys represent trust and identity.

8
00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:53.954
A certificate reference such as ExampleCertificateName may identify the material used to authenticate a service or establish trust.

9
00:00:53.954 --> 00:01:03.157
The private key behind that reference must be protected, access-controlled, rotated according to policy, and kept out of logs and exports.

10
00:01:03.157 --> 00:01:06.959
Signing keys and encryption keys are high-impact secrets.

11
00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:11.360
Signing material can determine whether data or tokens are trusted.

12
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.229
Encryption material can protect confidentiality or unlock protected data.

13
00:01:16.229 --> 00:01:24.899
These secrets require ownership, rotation plans, backup or recovery decisions, and careful separation from unrelated environments.

14
00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:30.767
API keys, service tokens, and webhook secrets often act as bearer-style access material.

15
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:35.903
Whoever can present the value may be treated as authorized by another system.

16
00:01:35.903 --> 00:01:44.639
That makes accidental display, logging, browser exposure, support exports, or reuse across unrelated environments especially risky.

17
00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:47.974
Different secret types require different handling.

18
00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:59.512
Authentication secrets, encryption material, signing material, integration credentials, and storage access each have different rotation, backup, scope, and monitoring needs.

19
00:01:59.512 --> 00:02:06.181
Avoid custom cryptographic handling and prefer platform-supported mechanisms and reviewed libraries.
